# Commit messages

When asked to propose or write a commit message, follow this.

## Inspect changes

1. Run `git status` and `git diff --cached` (or `git diff` if nothing is staged).
2. Summarize the staged diff. Mention only files and behavior that are in it.

## Format

Output one plaintext fenced block containing the full message only, ready to paste into vim or pass to `git commit -F -`.

- Subject: one line, about 50 characters, at most 72. Imperative.
- Blank line, then body bullets hard-wrapped at 72 characters.

```
type(scope): brief description

- Change 1
- Change 2 that runs long and continues on the next
  line, indented under the bullet text
```

`type` is `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `perf`, or `chore`. `scope` is the area: `analysis`, `core`, `schedulers`, `dsp`, `lagrange`, `noise`, `systems`, `plotting`, `flattop`, `utils`, `tests`, `docs`, `benchmarks`.

Leave a URL, path, or formula intact on its own line when wrapping it would be worse.

## Traceability

When the change continues a handoff, cite it in the body, for example `Handoff: docs/handoffs/20260905_noise-generator-normalization.md`.

## Repo policy

Follow `.cursor/rules/git-workflow.mdc`.

- Drafting a message does not include `git add` or `git commit`.
- Create the commit only when the user asked for one, or when they invoked a commit command (`commit-step`, `commit-all-focused`).
